{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_012PDdsFwh1LLikqj3KKdYUv","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I have a good picture of what needs to be implemented. Let me look at the existing code:\n\n1. **`config.rs`**: Already has `picoquic_config_get_option_char_index` and `picoquic_config_get_option_name_index`. The missing function is `picoquic_config_get_command_line_option_index` which combines both. Looking at the C code:\n   - If `opt_string[0] == '-'` and `opt_string[1] != 0`:\n     - If `opt_string[2] == 0` (single char): call `picoquic_config_get_option_char_index(opt_string[1])`\n     - Else if `opt_string[1] == '-'` and `opt_string[2] != 0` (long option): call `picoquic_config_get_option_name_index` with the option name\n   - Otherwise return -1.\n\nI notice there's already a `parse_option_string` private function that handles most of this logic, so I just need to wrap it in the public `picoquic_config_get_command_line_option_index` function that returns an `i32` index.\n\nLooking at the existing Rust implementation...\n\nExamining the existing function signatures...\n\nThe C version handles the same logic but with explicit character-by-character checks: it validates the dash prefix, then branches on whether it's a short option (single character after dash) or long option (double dash followed by name). The Rust version achieves the same result more concisely using pattern matching and string slicing. The string length checks in Rust map correctly to the C logic — `len() == 2` for short options like `-x` and `starts_with(\"--\") && len() > 2` for long options like `--name`. So `parse_option_string` already handles this correctly, and I just need to wrap it to return an `i32` index instead of the tuple.\n\nNow I'm looking at what's missing in `cubic.rs` — the file has the core state and observation methods, but I need to implement the constructor, recovery handling, spurious loss correction, and notification callbacks that the C code uses with connection and path types. Let me check how the Rust bindings define those C types and what the notification system looks like.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":24719,"cache_read_input_tokens":30617,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":24719},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f89f9153-c014-4310-b972-78fe66d716ab","uuid":"5d0c3b2b-7192-4bb6-9e70-22a340645a19"}
